Great game, great devs!
, apparently, it seems just to catch up the nostalgic ones on duty from the NES 8-bit era, but it is much more than that. The devs worked to get only the best parts of the 8-bit era (chiptune music, 2D graphics, etc.) and updated the old ones (fullscreen, more colors, etc.), but the 8-bit era is not the only inspiration in this game.
The game has (for now) three campaigns, which each one is a completely different game. During the gameplay several inspirations from older games can be seen:
* a map screen navigation with random enemies that may appear (from
);
* each level has a boss in the end (like in the
series);
* Shovel Knight can use their shovel to dig, attack (similar to the sword attack of Ryu Hayabusa from
) or do a pogo jump (like Scrooge McDuck do in
or Link do in
).
However, the game has some other new interesting things like:
* There's no life system, instead, it's more like
. You'll lose part of your gold, but you can recover if you reach the same spot without die, otherwise, you'll lose forever that gold and will have to recover this new part that you lost;
* Most of 8-bit and 16-bit platforming games are famous for being hard.
instead has a more gradual difficulty, it's fair and the game is never trying to trick you - all of the deaths that I've experienced is my own fault. The game is not that easy, but is not impossible too, each level was crafted to adjust a perfect balance between fun and fair dificulty. This point make the game have a high replay value.
has an amazing soundtrack! Composed by Jake Kaufman (who made an excelent work in
, for example) and some contributions by Manami Matsumae (from
, Air Man stage in
, Nitro Man stage in
, etc.).
This game has more features like:
* Local co-op mode (only in Shovel Knight's campaign);
* Body-swap (only in Shovel Knight's campaign);
* Battletoads stage (only in Shovel Knight's campaign);
* Challenge mode;
* Two new expansions coming for FREE for who has this game!
In these days, I've never seen an amazing dedication to own work and with the community as what Yacht Club Games has done with
, a rare example of great management makes that all the people from YCG deserves the praises and
is a worth buy game.
Overall: 10/10

Awesome game. It's like a light version of Cave Story. You still can upgrade your weapons and health, but levels are linear and there are just 7 of them. But after completing the game you unlock "Zangyou" mode, which is not only harder, but also kinda continues the main story.
- Gameplay is great;
- Sound is great;
- Pixel art is great.
If you like Cave Story then you probably would like Kero Blaster as well. 7/10

Very nice game. Buy it if you like:
1. Warhammer 40k lore;
2. Good turn based strategy mechanics;
3. Miniatures.
Don't buy if you want a great cinematic story driven campaign. The game doesn't have one.
Sanctus Reach is an indie title done right.

I personally find the game very fun. Performance isn't great on my system. The AI drivers can also be dumb as... very nice people. However, I much prefer this game over the nearest rival, OMSI. It's a lot easier to get into playing. Overall I'd rate this game as a 7/10. Only drawbacks for me are the AI who fail to observe road laws in some scenarios; and also the performance. I'm netting 30-40 fps in cities whilst running high settings. Very little gain when dropping from ultra.
I must admit this game is absolutely stunning visually. Definitely worth a look into for any simulator fan. I personally wouldn't pay full price for the game however.
